 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187969 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187969 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92c91297-c7c0-5bdc-b9d9-a9a52ae806c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187970 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187970 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187970 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "99b215f4-c062-59cc-b003-ea6ef57c1e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187971 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187971 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187971 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b7165ef5-c1ee-52e1-97ed-92ea885bd55a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187972 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187972 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187972 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0874d691-cb56-565c-9d14-cf75209b28ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187973 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187973 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187973 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22fb8de2-7841-5738-b5c1-945f57d8818f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188204 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188204 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3.$ If $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=1,$ then what is $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188204 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b196d96-a95f-53d6-a0c5-7233de14a401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188204 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+1 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(T) & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188207 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188207 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$, then $\\textrm{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(T)=3$\n\n- $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=3$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0eddf8b0-bdd9-55db-9388-d39bd91c24c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188207 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^4$ into $\\mathbb{R}^4,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/c0/c09c9b85da2142dda499df51a25870f0d80360fad7f78e501f26c400128bcce6.png) \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ and the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\textrm{rank}(T)=\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 4$ (or since $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 0$), the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188216 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188216 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$ given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{ [ x, y, z ]^T \\, : \\, x=y=z\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91885df0-9f1d-5c18-acf1-70259cf7f4d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188216 #edb/ref 1074 "Any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}.$ Therefore, $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188261 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188273 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188281 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1045 "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188448 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e8e2dcd1-d239-5120-a71f-5337f9c0fb01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477611 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186188911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477612 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186188911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477613 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186188911 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188911 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188911 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- **III.** $T$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186188911 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e86ac40c-9f96-593f-88c6-aef65bbc3b17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188911 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188924 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,-x+z=0}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09a30319-539f-5865-a29f-fd523154b963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188924 #edb/ref 1074 "Solving $-x+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=x.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188981 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T: R^{7} → R^{7}$. If $rank(T) = 3$ then $nullity(T) =$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "710bfb29-d88f-5f8e-82d2-27ffc4845463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188982 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $rank(T)$ given that $T: R^{4} → R^{4}$ and\n$Ker(T) = {[x, y, z, w]^{T}: x + y + z = 0, 2x - y - w = 0}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f05e62e2-1597-5312-8f1a-20a85ca4f47f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189060 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189060 #edb/ref 1061 20021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189060 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b289521-18f1-582d-b604-520f11ba29d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189060 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189077 #edb/ref 1061 26827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189077 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "626e026a-01da-503f-a127-2b353dbc209b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189077 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189078 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189078 #edb/ref 1061 26840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189078 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e0c933b-d880-5bfa-804a-944d21dae8a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189078 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189088 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189088 #edb/ref 1061 37613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189088 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6735a644-2990-5e5c-b639-afb52f0b4d15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189088 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189365 #edb/ref 1064 "The **rank-nullity theorem in terms of linear transformations** states the following:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation, then* $$\\textrm{dim(Im}(\\mathbf{T})) + \\textrm{dim(Ker}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),$$ *or equivalently,* $$\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n.$$\n\nFor example, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation such that $$\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{ [ x, y, z ]^T \\, : \\, y+2z=0\\}.$$\n\nLet's use the rank-nullity theorem to compute $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})$ and $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$\n\nFirst, solving $y+2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y = -2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as $$\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis means that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}.$ And since $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are linearly independent, we have $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=2.$\n\nFinally, using the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain $$\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\ & =3-2 \\\\ & =1.\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189365 #edb/ref 1065 4379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189365 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9c4215af-a9cf-5a1a-aabc-40c5395f2df4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189365 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365074 #edb/ref 17592186