6 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366146 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366146 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "319ca848-ea15-555c-8b75-4d09284cc695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366147 #edb/ref 1080 "Not enough information." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366147 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366147 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61e472bd-ec33-5a3e-8b54-62a2891a9c10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366148 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366148 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366148 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5dc371b-1396-5e61-a5b7-e3d432e28bc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366149 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366149 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366149 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eca1d3e3-4905-5d15-ac5c-4cc6e7addee7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366150 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366150 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366150 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dc4ff1b0-90ec-51f1-9adc-f5e7ecc391d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366151 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366151 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366151 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f218d39c-ce4c-5b79-8c33-1b7543b0b540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452770 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452771 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452772 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366455 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84063" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366455 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366455 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n- **II.** If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y-2z=0,x+z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n- **III.** If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c81c0d34-9745-5e75-9229-e0ad0c633344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366455 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1045 "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186406847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406809 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1a6d8485-0053-526d-9b37-3def23fa3c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406810 #edb/ref 1080 "Not enough information." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406810 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406810 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f3fa2ee-eac1-54f7-a4c6-ec5b539510f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406812 #edb/ref 1080 "Not enough information." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406812 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406812 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1470852b-03e9-5db4-8d33