itten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ -x-y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473754 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473755 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473756 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406843 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ * \n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$\n- **III.** $T$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b72abcb6-85da-5c4d-a8eb-72ce20ae6b72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406843 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406844 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406844 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b0b57c7-2d59-5d20-9983-005e3257de52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406844 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406845 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406845 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81a2fa92-8011-53d7-b83d-7c048c758080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406845 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406846 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83597e6d-f7ab-5d6b-86d3-62716c58dc6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406846 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406847 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406847 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "962a6c24-9fbc-5081-8aa6-b1e292aead4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406847 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a96e7cad-642a-5c4d-add9-f21b4c3f7cb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1